- How would you test edge cases (0, all ones, alternating patterns)?
- How does endianness affect results across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of non-trivial bit rearrangements across 128 bits without loops. The task blends endian concepts with mask-shift patterns, common in low-level data handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit data as hi/lo halves
- Byte-wise reversal using masks and shifts
- Endianness-aware data transformations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline (actual C-like code uses uint128 struct)
static uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t t = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  t = ((t & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((t & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  return (t << 32) | (t >> 32);
}
uint128 endianSwap128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = bswap64(x.lo); r.lo = bswap64(x.hi); return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable endianness at compile time? 
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

